232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The management of piggery waste has emerged as a critical issue in India, where the rapid intensification of piggery operations has raised significant environmental and public health concerns. This section seeks to analyse findings from a study conducted among piggery units in India, aimed at examining various aspects of waste management practices followed in the piggeries across the country, focusing on the implications of these practices for local ecosystems and communities. Through the comprehensive study, data was collected on several key factors related to waste disposal methods and their associated risks. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0 1]
>>
endobj
233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The study addressed critical issues such as the disposal of untreated wastewater and slurry, which can lead to severe environmental pollution when discarded in drains or agricultural fields. Additionally, the presence of slurry pits was examined as they may create breeding grounds for disease vectors, particularly mosquitoes, which pose risks for vector-borne diseases. The absence of infrastructure for treating solid waste was also assessed, alongside practices related to the disposal of solid waste in water bodies and drains. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
234 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Furthermore, the study evaluated the mechanisms in place for managing biomedical waste generated from veterinary practices associated with piggery operations. The potential for fly infestations and odour nuisances due to inadequate waste management was also documented, as these issues can significantly affect the quality of life for nearby residents. The study also considered carcass disposal methods and whether siting parameters met environmental and health standards. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (By analysing these factors, this study aims to provide a clearer understanding of the current state of piggery waste management in India and its implications for public health and environmental sustainability. The findings will contribute to ongoing discussions about improving waste management practices in the livestock sector, ultimately promoting better health outcomes for communities and protecting local ecosystems.)
/Pg 14 0 R
>>
endobj
